<Base lang="en" title="Digital Life Certificate 2026: Submit from Home by Face Scan in the November Campaign — No Treasury Visit Needed" description="The National Digital Life Certificate campaign is running in November 2026 — pensioners and family pensioners can submit the life certificate from home using the Jeevan Pramaan app and Aadhaar Face RD face scan. The full process, the details you need, and why to keep your Pramaan ID safe." path="/en/blog/digital-life-certificate-jeevan-pramaan/" altPath="/blog/digital-life-certificate-jeevan-pramaan/" faqs={faqs} updated="22 November 2026" datePublished="2026-11-22" dateModified="2026-11-22">
<div class="content-col"><h1 class="page-title">Digital Life Certificate 2026: submit from home by face scan in the November campaign</h1>
<p class="updated-line">Amar Ujala, Jaunpur, 5 November 2026 — as told by Senior Treasury Officer Uma Shankar Yadav · 22 November 2026</p>
<figure class="blog-featured"><img src="/images/blog/digital-life-certificate-jeevan-pramaan-featured.webp" alt="Digital Life Certificate — submitting from home by face scan on a mobile (example illustration)" /><figcaption>Example illustration — edistrictupseva.com</figcaption></figure>
<p>There is welcome news for pensioners in Uttar Pradesh in November 2026. On the directions of the Government of India and the Government of Uttar Pradesh, the <strong>National Digital Life Certificate campaign</strong> has begun — pensioners and family pensioners no longer have to make rounds of the treasury office to submit the life certificate. They can now submit the digital life certificate from home, on their own mobile phone. Senior Treasury Officer Uma Shankar Yadav of Jaunpur has said that pensioners can download the Aadhaar Face RD and Jeevan Pramaan apps on their mobile and send the digital life certificate through a face scan. This article is based on that news report — what the campaign is, the full process of submitting from a mobile, which details to keep ready, and why the Pramaan ID must be kept safe after submitting.</p>
<section class="box"><h2>The November 2026 campaign — what is changing</h2><p>Every year a pensioner has to prove that they are alive — that is what the life certificate is for. Earlier, this meant going to the treasury or the bank, standing in a queue, and for elderly pensioners that was the hardest part of the job. In the November 2026 campaign, the same work can be done from home on a mobile. According to the Senior Treasury Officer, once the digital life certificate is submitted, the pensioner or family pensioner will not need to come to the treasury. A help desk has also been set up at the treasury office, staffed by post office employees, for the convenience of pensioners — so a pensioner who cannot manage the mobile process will still get help at the treasury. The campaign covers pensioners and family pensioners alike, and the Senior Treasury Officer has appealed to as many pensioners as possible to use this digital facility.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>How to submit from a mobile — step by step</h2><p>Submitting the digital life certificate from a mobile takes five steps. First, download two apps on your mobile — the <strong>Jeevan Pramaan app and the Aadhaar Face RD app</strong>. Download both apps only from the official app store. Then open the Jeevan Pramaan app and enter your <strong>Aadhaar number and mobile number</strong>, and verify with the <strong>OTP</strong> that arrives on the mobile. Next, fill in the details in the form — the pensioner’s name, pension type, department details, PPO number, bank account number and IFSC code. For a Uttar Pradesh state pensioner, the sanctioning authority is the <strong>State Government of Uttar Pradesh</strong>, the disbursing agency is the <strong>UP Treasury</strong>, and the treasury is <strong>your own district</strong>. After filling in the details, the last step is the <strong>face scan</strong> — the mobile camera scans your face and the certificate is submitted. As soon as it is submitted you receive a <strong>Pramaan ID</strong> — keep it safe, because it is how you check whether the certificate was submitted or rejected.</p><figure class="shot"><img src="/images/blog/life-certificate-steps.svg" alt="Digital Life Certificate — five steps to submit from home" /><figcaption>Five steps to submit from home — edistrictupseva.com</figcaption></figure><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Detail</th><th>What to enter</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Aadhaar number</td><td>The pensioner’s Aadhaar number</td></tr><tr><td>Mobile number</td><td>The number linked to Aadhaar — the OTP comes to this number</td></tr><tr><td>Pensioner’s name</td><td>The name as per the PPO</td></tr><tr><td>Pension type</td><td>Select your pension type</td></tr><tr><td>Department details</td><td>The department the pension comes from</td></tr><tr><td>PPO number</td><td>The Pension Payment Order number</td></tr><tr><td>Bank account number + IFSC</td><td>The account the pension goes into</td></tr><tr><td>Sanctioning authority</td><td>State Government of Uttar Pradesh</td></tr><tr><td>Disbursing agency</td><td>UP Treasury</td></tr><tr><td>Treasury</td><td>Your own district</td></tr></tbody></table></section>
<section class="box"><h2>If you cannot use a mobile — other routes</h2><p>Not every pensioner has a smartphone, and not everyone knows how to use apps — the campaign has arranged for that too. The facility can also be taken at the nearest <strong>post office, Jan Seva Kendra (CSC) or cyber café</strong> — the staff there will help submit the certificate. When taking help at a Jan Seva Kendra, never share your OTP or password with anyone — the precautions for using a centre are in our <a href="/en/csc-guide/">CSC guide</a>. There is also a help desk at the treasury office, staffed by post office employees, where a pensioner can go for help. Whichever route you use, make sure you take the Pramaan ID at the end and keep it safe — it is the identity of the certificate you submitted.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Keep the Pramaan ID safe — how to check the status</h2><p>Submitting the certificate is not the last step — it is equally important to check whether it was accepted or rejected. The Senior Treasury Officer has appealed to all pensioners to keep their <strong>life certificate ID (Pramaan ID)</strong> safe after submitting the digital life certificate, so that they can know whether the certificate was submitted or rejected. The status of the certificate can be checked on the Jeevan Pramaan portal with the Pramaan ID. If the status shows “rejected”, something was wrong in the face scan or the details — submit again with the correct details, or take help from the treasury help desk. The general way to read the status of any government application — what to look for in the status and what a remark means — is explained in our <a href="/en/status-verification/">Status &amp; Verification guide</a>.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Keep these ready before you submit</h2><ul class="checklist"><li><strong>Aadhaar number</strong> — and the mobile number linked to Aadhaar, on which the OTP will arrive.</li><li><strong>PPO number</strong> — the Pension Payment Order number; you will find it on the pension book or earlier papers.</li><li><strong>Bank account number and IFSC code</strong> — enter the details of the same account the pension goes into.</li><li><strong>Pension type and department details</strong> — decide in advance which department the pension comes from.</li><li><strong>A well-lit place</strong> — for the face scan, light should fall straight on the face.</li><li><strong>A paper or diary to write the Pramaan ID in</strong> — you will receive the ID as soon as the certificate is submitted, so write it down at once.</li></ul></section>
<section class="box"><h2>If something goes wrong</h2><p>If the face scan does not work, try again in good light, with your face straight and the camera held steady — a scan often fails in darkness or with strong light behind you. If the OTP does not arrive, check the mobile number and the network, wait a while and ask for it again. If the details were entered wrongly, the certificate can be rejected — check the PPO number and bank details once more before filling them in. If the problem keeps coming back, take help from the treasury office help desk or the nearest post office. Never give your Aadhaar number, OTP or bank details to an unknown person or website — submitting a life certificate needs no fee and no agent. If the problem is with an eDistrict certificate, the right order of contact — check the status first, then the helpdesk, then a complaint — is in our <a href="/en/blog/edistrict-helpline-contact/">helpline guide</a>.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Sources</h2><p>This article is based on the <strong>Amar Ujala, Jaunpur, 5 November 2026</strong> news report, in which Senior Treasury Officer Uma Shankar Yadav described the National Digital Life Certificate campaign of November 2026 and the process of submitting from home. That report gives no last date for the campaign — so this article says only “November campaign”; confirm the last date with your treasury or on the Jeevan Pramaan portal. The report also gives no pension amount or beneficiary figure, so no amount is stated here. For more guides, see the <a href="/en/">eDistrict UP homepage</a>.</p></section>
